  "overview": {
    "text": "Escherichia coli (E. coli) is a Gram-negative, rod-shaped bacterium commonly found in the lower intestine of warm-blooded animals. Most strains do not cause illness, but certain strains have traits that let them cause disease, including diarrhea; Shiga toxin-producing strains can sometimes lead to serious kidney complications. E. coli is also important in science and biotechnology: researchers can manipulate it readily, grow it quickly and inexpensively, and use its well-studied genetics and available laboratory tools to produce recombinant proteins. Its distinguishing significance is this contrast: the same bacterial species includes ordinary gut inhabitants, disease-causing strains, and laboratory strains useful for research and production.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68004926",
      "https://www.cdc.gov/ecoli/php/technical-info/index.html",
      "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15607230/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "E. coli is a Gram-negative, facultatively anaerobic, rod-shaped bacterium.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68004926"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It commonly lives in the gastrointestinal tract, and most strains do not cause illness.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/ecoli/php/technical-info/index.html",
        "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68004926"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Disease-causing strains differ in their virulence traits; Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, particularly some O157 strains, can cause hemolytic uremic syndrome, which can damage the kidneys.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.cdc.gov/ecoli/php/technical-info/index.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Certain extraintestinal pathogenic strains cause urinary tract infections; CDC reports that E. coli causes 80–90% of UTIs.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://cdc.gov/uti/hcp/clinical-overview/index.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "E. coli is widely used to produce recombinant proteins because it is relatively easy to manipulate and grow, and researchers have many compatible laboratory tools and strains.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15607230/"
      ]
    }
  ],
